      {
        "id": 177325,
        "name": "MigrantP",
        "username": "MigrantP",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/migrantp/{size}/1921_2.png",
        "created_at": "2016-06-09T16:27:00.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>Bachelor of Mathematics reporting for duty! Let's hope I don't screw this up.</p><p>At the start of the game, Wager Master puts H condition cards into play. There are 4 copies of Wagelings in a 25 card deck. So if H is 3 or 4, he can win by having H of them on top of the deck when it's initially shuffled.</p><p>There's a 4 in 25 chance he plays a Wageling first. P = (4/25) = 16% chance</p><p>There's a 3 in 24 chance he also plays a second one. P = (3/24) * (4/25) = 2% chance</p><p>There's a 2 in 23 chance he also plays a third one. P = (2/23) * (3/24) * (4/25) ~=&nbsp;0.1739% chance</p><p><span>There's a 1 in 22 chance he also plays a fourth one. P = (1/22) *&nbsp;</span><span>(2/23) * (3/24) * (4/25)&nbsp;</span><span>~=&nbsp;</span><span>0.007905% chance</span></p><p>So the odds are 1 in 575 for H=3, and 1 in 12,650 for H=4.</p><p>edit: Oh, I did screw it up, it's more complicated than that. \"Cards are revealed from the top of the villain deck until H condition cards are revealed and put into play.\" That's more math, I'll make another post.</p>",
        "post_number": 30,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T05:01:19.573Z",
